  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nConvective development has been slow to materialize across \neastern Idaho today. Extensive morning and early afternoon cloud\ncover effectively capped diurnal heating, suppressing overall \nthermodynamic instability. However, recent radar trends indicate\nthat the cloud cap is beginning to erode, with isolated to \nscattered convective initiation now underway over the higher \nterrain. While recent high-resolution convective-allowing models\n(CAMs)\u2014including the latest HRRR runs\u2014have noticeably dialed \nback forecast precipitation totals and overall storm coverage, \nthe ambient atmosphere remains abnormally moisture-laden by \nlocal standards. With rich precipitable water values still \nentrenched across the region, any slow-moving thunderstorm that \ndevelops through this evening will remain capable of producing \nhigh rainfall rates and localized flash flooding. \n\nFrom a geographic standpoint, the strongest model signal for \nthunderstorm development through tonight targets the Central \nMountains. The signal is comparatively weaker across the Eastern\nHighlands\u2014where a Flood Watch remains in effect\u2014but even \nisolated storm development there could quickly prove problematic\ngiven high rainfall efficiency. Outside of convection, daytime \nmaximum temperatures today have been significantly suppressed \ncompared to recent days due to cloud shading. \n\nThis reprieve from the heat will be short-lived. Heat begins \nreturning on Thursday as high-pressure ridging amplifies over \nthe region and drier air pushes in from the southwest. While \nsufficient lingering moisture will remain in place to support \nlow-end shower and storm chances across the higher terrain on \nThursday, the vast majority of eastern Idaho will begin \ntransitioning toward a dry, typical late-summer regime. \n\nBy Friday and continuing through much of next week, strong\nupper-level ridging will firmly lock into place across the\nIntermountain West. Widespread dry conditions will prevail for\nlower elevations, though isolated, terrain-forced afternoon\nshowers or storms cannot be entirely ruled out on any given day.\nThe primary headline heading into the weekend and next week will\nbe persistent, unseasonably high heat. Afternoon maximum\ntemperatures across lower valley locations\u2014including the Snake\nPlain and Magic Valley\u2014are increasingly likely to climb into the\nmid-to-upper 90s, with a few localized spots approaching the\n100-degree mark.",
